# Line endings are pinned so git never rewrites a file on disk after it was built.
#
# With core.autocrlf=true and no rules here, git converted LF to CRLF in the working tree
# at commit time. That mutated the source *after* the DLL had been compiled from it, so a
# SHA-256 comparison of source against the deployed binary could disagree for a reason that
# had nothing to do with the build. It cost a wrong diagnosis once — the cause was first
# blamed on incremental build state — and the workaround was to fix the order of operations
# to commit, clean, build, test, deploy, hash.
#
# That order is still correct, but it should not be load-bearing. Pinning eol removes the
# rewrite entirely, and with it the "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warnings that would
# otherwise bury a genuine warning in noise.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.cs      text eol=lf diff=csharp
*.csproj  text eol=lf
*.sln     text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.log     text eol=lf
*.gitkeep text eol=lf

# Windows shells break on LF continuation, so these keep CRLF deliberately.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

*.dll binary
*.pdb binary
*.png binary
*.docx binary
